{"parentUuid":"31173a66-2362-4f25-87bc-31f94694fb94","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"2576205b-fb1a-4a1e-b6a8-60e5e8c900b4","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"ad1cd98","slug":"jaunty-yawning-boot","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929","id":"msg_vrtx_01BD2u19R4rj4s7B6R7t5vB3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_01SpuV6ysParku3oHzNqhzcA","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/full_text.txt","offset":0,"limit":200}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":862,"cache_read_input_tokens":28125,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":862,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXb86oKkbpXNnGbRSrhbY","type":"assistant","uuid":"e40dedfa-49d0-4cd3-a36d-3955555ad544","timestamp":"2026-01-29T05:33:05.616Z"}
{"parentUuid":"e40dedfa-49d0-4cd3-a36d-3955555ad544","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"2576205b-fb1a-4a1e-b6a8-60e5e8c900b4","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"ad1cd98","slug":"jaunty-yawning-boot","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01SpuV6ysParku3oHzNqhzcA","type":"tool_result","content":"     0→Temporally multiplexed ion-photon quantum interface via fast ion-chain transport\n     1→Bingran You,1, 2 Qiming Wu,1, 2,∗David Miron,1, 2 Wenjun Ke,1\n     2→Inder Monga,2 Erhan Saglamyurek,1, 2 and Hartmut Haeffner1, 2,†\n     3→1Department of Physics, University of California, Berkeley, CA 94270, USA\n     4→2Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory, Berkeley, CA 94720, USA\n     5→(Dated: January 12, 2026)\n     6→High-rate remote entanglement between photon and matter-based qubits is essential for dis-\n     7→tributed quantum information processing. A key technique to increase the modest entangling rates\n     8→of existing long-distance quantum networking approaches is multiplexing. Here, we demonstrate\n     9→a temporally multiplexed ion-photon interface via rapid transport of a chain of nine calcium ions\n    10→across 74µm within 86µs. The non-classical nature of the multiplexed photons is verified by mea-\n    11→suring the second-order correlation function with an average value ofg(2)(0) = 0.060(13), indicating\n    12→negligible crosstalk between the multiplexed modes. In addition, we characterize the motional\n    13→degree-of-freedom of the ion crystal after transport and find that it is coherently excited to as much\n    14→as ¯nα ≈ 110 for the center-of-mass mode. Our proof-of-principle implementation paves the way\n    15→for large-scale quantum networking with trapped ions, but highlights some challenges that must be\n    16→overcome.\n    17→Remote entanglement across distant quantum nodes [?\n    18→? ] may be used for long-distance quantum key distribu-\n    19→tion [? ? ], modular quantum computer architectures [?\n    20→? ], as well as quantum enhanced metrology and sens-\n    21→ing [? ? ? ]. Light-matter quantum interfaces are a fun-\n    22→damental building block for such applications, and allow\n    23→for distributed entanglement between stationary matter\n    24→qubits by using \"flying\" photons.\n    25→For practical purposes, these quantum interfaces need\n    26→to be capable of establishing remote entanglement at\n    27→high rates across a large-scale network of quantum nodes.\n    28→However, in widely adopted probabilistic schemes based\n    29→on heralded photon detection [? ? ? ], it is infeasible to\n    30→distribute entanglement at useful rates over a few kilo-\n    31→meters, as the attempt rate is limited by the round-trip\n    32→travel-time of photons in a single mode. For example, for\n    33→a communication distance of 10km, photon-travel time\n    34→will be around 100 µs, meaning that the attempt can-\n    35→not be higher than 10kHz. In this scenario, even with\n    36→use of a state-of-the-art photon-matter interface that can\n    37→yield close to unity photon extraction efficiency [? ], the\n    38→matter-photon entanglement rate beyond tens of kilome-\n    39→ters would be limited to sub 10s−1 with total loss around\n    40→15dBafterconsideringthecollectiveprobabilityofgener-\n    41→ating and detecting telecom photons at each node, along\n    42→with the optical fiber loss [? ? ].\n    43→A key solution to this challenge is multiplexing: to\n    44→combine multiple signals into a single channel and there-\n    45→fore increase the attempt rate [? ? ]. Multiplexing has\n    46→become a mature technology for ensemble-based quan-\n    47→tum interfaces, both in atomic gases and rare-earth ion\n    48→doped solid-state systems [? ? ? ? ]. However, large-\n    49→scale local quantum information processing is technically\n    50→challenging in these platforms [? ].\n    51→In contrast, single emitters, including trapped ions and\n    52→neutral atoms, offer excellent local quantum information\n    53→processing capability beside their natural interface with\n    54→light at convenient wavelengths for quantum frequency\n    55→conversion (QFC) [? ? ], and the possibility of long-\n    56→lived storage of entanglement [? ? ]. On the other hand,\n    57→implementing a multiplexed light-matter interface with\n    58→these systems is technically challenging. Towards over-\n    59→coming this problem, a few multiplexing schemes have\n    60→already been proposed for ion and atom-based quantum\n    61→processors [? ? ? ? ]. The only reported experimental\n    62→work, we are aware of, is the demonstration of multi-\n    63→plexing using a static three-ion chain [? ]. In view of the\n    64→recent advances of the quantum CCD architecture [? ? ?\n    65→], a complementary approach to multiplexing is the pro-\n    66→cess of ion-transport through a specific spatial location\n    67→with maximized photon coupling efficiency.\n    68→In this work, we demonstrate a temporal multiplexing\n    69→schemebasedonthetransportofanion-chainforimprov-\n    70→ing the rate of ion-photon entanglement over long dis-\n    71→tances. In our experiments, we generate on-demand sin-\n    72→gle photons by shuttling a nine-ion chain across the focus\n    73→of a single-ion addressing beam. This scheme is expected\n    74→to lead to a nearly nine-fold increase in attempt rate of\n    75→the entanglement generation for quantum repeater nodes\n    76→separated by >100 km. We verify the single-photon na-\n    77→ture of the photon trains by measuring a second-order\n    78→time correlation of g(2)(0) = 0.060(13) without back-\n    79→ground subtraction. Furthermore, we address the prob-\n    80→lem of motional excitation during the transport, which\n    81→is detrimental to local entangling operations [? ] and\n    82→in the case of using a cavity for stimulating the photons\n    83→would lead to uncertainly in the coupling strength. [?\n    84→]. Using a shuttling function designed to mitigate mo-\n    85→tional excitation, we find coherent excitation of¯nα∼50\n    86→on the center-of-mass (COM) mode during one round of\n    87→ion chain transport. These results show that the pro-\n    88→posed multiplexing scheme can be scaled up to higher\n    89→rates provided that more optimal transport methods are\n    90→applied.\n    91→2\n    92→The schematics of the experimental procedures is illus-\n    93→trated in Fig.??(a). Our experiment is conducted using\n    94→anRFPaultrap, composedoffourRFbladesforgenerat-\n    95→ing radial pseudopotential and two DC endcap electrodes\n    96→for providing an axial harmonic confinement. We typi-\n    97→cally trap a string of nine40Ca+ ions in a linear configu-\n    98→ration with the COM mode frequency ofωx,z/(2π) =×\n    99→{1.15, 0.179} MHz and Lamb-Dicke (LD) parameters of\n   100→the axial modes, ranging between 0.09 and 0.23 on the\n   101→42S1/2↔32D5/2 transition. Two global laser beams at\n   102→397nm and 866nm (not shown in the figure) evenly il-\n   103→luminate the entire ion chain for Doppler cooling and\n   104→optical pumping, and a tightly focused addressing beam\n   105→at 866nm allows for resonant excitation to extract the\n   106→397nm single photons from individual ions. The gener-\n   107→ated single photons are collected by an objective with\n   108→a numerical aperture of NA=0.3 (collection efficiency\n   109→Pc≈2.5%) and directed to a 50/50 beam splitter (BS).\n   110→At the exit port of BS, photons are detected by two pho-\n   111→tomultiplier tube (PMT)-based single-photon detectors\n   112→(SPD), and their arrival times are recorded with a time-\n   113→tagger for subsequent analysis.\n   114→We generate on-demand single-photons based on a\n   115→background-free scheme, as illustrated in Fig. ??(b) [?\n   116→]. In this process, we first Doppler cool (detuning\n   117→∆ = −Γ/2) the ion chain for 200µs, with the first 100µs\n   118→assisted by another beam−500MHz detuned from the\n   119→42S1/2↔42P1/2 transition (not shown) to mitigate col-\n   120→lision induced ion chain melting in the Paul trap [? ].\n   121→Then we begin the photon generation sequences with\n   122→optical pumping to the 32D3/2 state for 3 µs, followed\n   123→by transport of the chain to position each ion in the\n   124→tight focus of the 866nm addressing beam resonant with\n   125→32D3/2 ↔42P1/2 transition to generate 397nm single\n   126→photons (see Fig.??(c)).\n   127→In the ion-chain transport process, the endcap volt-\n   128→ages are controlled by an arbitrary waveform generator\n   129→(AWG) amplified by a custom-made, low-noise amplifier\n   130→circuit with a gain of ten through low-pass filters and\n   131→a vacuum feedthrough. The low-pass filters have cutoff\n   132→frequencies of 1.9MHz to allow fast transport of the ion\n   133→chain close to the speed of the COM mode frequency.\n   134→The programmed and the measured waveform show a\n   135→negligible latency effect from the filters (Fig.??(a)). The\n   136→forward shuttling function has eight steps, during each of\n   137→which a different ion is placed in the focus of the address-\n   138→ing beam for 1.7µs with the beam turned on simultane-\n   139→ously. After completing this sequence, we move the entire\n   140→ion chain back to the original position in 35µs using the\n   141→same function form in one step. The voltage ramping\n   142→function on the two endcapsV1,2(t) is in the form of a\n   143→sigmoid-like polynomial function such that the first and\n   144→second order derivative at the beginning and the end of\n   145→the transport vanish [? ].\n   146→d2xi\n   147→dt2 =−ω2\n   148→0xi + kq2\n   149→m\n   150→N∑\n   151→j=1\n   152→j̸=i\n   153→1\n   154→(xi−xj)2·sgn (xi−xj) (1)\n   155→where ∆V is the voltage difference between the be-\n   156→ginning and the end of a step,t is the time after the\n   157→end of the previous step, and T = 9.1 µs is the total\n   158→time of each transport. The details of voltage optimiza-\n   159→tion and numerical simulation of motional heating can\n   160→be found in [? ]. We reconstruct the temporal profile of\n   161→397nm photons during transport using the recorded ar-\n   162→rival times of photons on the PMTs. Fig??(b) shows the\n   163→emission from individual ions (modes). Data is accumu-\n   164→lated for 40min, during which around1.56×106 attempts\n   165→were made to the whole string, corresponding to attempt\n   166→rate 39.0kHz, an average photon extraction efficiency of\n   167→0.21% and single photons count rate of around 71cps.\n   168→Next, we perform a two-photon correlation experiment\n   169→to test the non-classical characteristics of our multiplexed\n   170→photon source [? ]. The probability of two-photon corre-\n   171→lationwhendetectingacorrelationeventontwodetectors\n   172→at different times is given by\n   173→ρc(τ) =ρ1(τ)ρ2(τ+δT), (2)\n   174→where ρ1(τ) and ρ2(τ+δT) are the probability of de-\n   175→tecting a photon att =τand τ+δTon detector 1 and\n   176→2. Fig. ??(a) shows the normalized correlation counts as\n   177→a function of the delay mode window. We choose a co-\n   178→incidence window of 300ns in each mode and measure\n   179→8 coincident counts at zero delay in 4.8 hours, corre-\n   180→sponding to g(2)(0) = 0.060(13). The residual correla-\n   181→tion can be explained by excitation of neighboring ions,\n   182→i.e., crosstalk of the addressing beam, which is sepa-\n   183→rately characterized to be 0.99 % using fluorescence of\n   184→the nine-ion chain on the camera, corresponding to ex-\n   185→pected averageg(2)\n   186→exp(0) = 0.049(8) (see Supplemental [?\n   187→]). To further verify this hypothesis, we repeat the mea-\n   188→surement with a single ion (Fig. ??(c)) and compare\n   189→it to addressing only the fifth ion in a static nine-ion\n   190→chain (Fig. ??(b)). The two experiments yieldg(2)(0) =\n   191→0.010(6) andg(2)(0) = 0.062(17) with 6.0 and 4.8 hours\n   192→of data accumulation, respectively. While the single ion\n   193→g2 is limited by the detector dark counts and ambient\n   194→light, the measurement of the static 9-ion chain g2 is\n   195→limited by the crosstalk of the addressing beam. The\n   196→results indicate the major source of residual correlation\n   197→is addressing crosstalk [? ]. This can be mitigated by\n   198→coupling the single photons into a single-mode fiber or\n   199→improving the optical quality of the excitation beam.\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"uuid":"610fecbf-eb37-4de3-bb56-58be83bd8264","timestamp":"2026-01-29T05:33:05.639Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e40dedfa-49d0-4cd3-a36d-3955555ad544"}
