{"parentUuid":"28cb53a1-9ce0-4f13-840c-138bbe9fde58","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"2576205b-fb1a-4a1e-b6a8-60e5e8c900b4","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"ad1cd98","slug":"jaunty-yawning-boot","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929","id":"msg_vrtx_016QBeV5J7wPDP7e7aRqSHY9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_013pmbG2tWLu8AmnGfvXDA1H","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/full_text.txt","offset":200,"limit":200}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3827,"cache_read_input_tokens":28987,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3827,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXb872FdEsw95rcba1JJ2","type":"assistant","uuid":"91c9a939-d39e-442c-9777-2d9eed02ce87","timestamp":"2026-01-29T05:33:09.086Z"}
{"parentUuid":"91c9a939-d39e-442c-9777-2d9eed02ce87","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"2576205b-fb1a-4a1e-b6a8-60e5e8c900b4","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"ad1cd98","slug":"jaunty-yawning-boot","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_013pmbG2tWLu8AmnGfvXDA1H","type":"tool_result","content":"   200→improving the optical quality of the excitation beam.\n   201→After characterizing the single-photon nature of the\n   202→transport-multiplexing scheme, we characterize the mo-\n   203→tion of the ions introduced by shuttling. This is impor-\n   204→tant as the quality of subsequent quantum operations\n   205→on the ions or ion-photon entanglement will depend on\n   206→3\n   207→the ions’ motional states. We further explore the sys-\n   208→temperformancebymeasuringthemotionalheatingfrom\n   209→the ion transport. To do this, we first perform side-\n   210→band cooling for all axial modes sequentially using the\n   211→method similar to that in [? ] and prepare the ion in\n   212→the state|↓⟩=|42S1/2,mJ =−1/2⟩. We compare the\n   213→|↓⟩↔|↑⟩=|32D5/2,mJ =−1/2⟩carrier transition be-\n   214→fore and after transport with a global 729nm beam along\n   215→the axial direction to determine how the transport af-\n   216→fects the ion-motion (Fig.??). The carrier Rabi flopping\n   217→is motional state sensitive, and the Hamiltonian has the\n   218→form of [? ? ]\n   219→Hi,M = (ℏ/2)Ω (i)σ(i)\n   220→x\n   221→M∏\n   222→m=1\n   223→exp\n   224→[\n   225→iηi,m\n   226→[\n   227→am +a†\n   228→m\n   229→)]\n   230→, (3)\n   231→where Ω (i) istheRabifrequencyofthe ithion, am anda†\n   232→m\n   233→are the creation and annihilation operators on themth\n   234→mode, andηi,m istheLDparameterofthe ithionandthe\n   235→mth mode. Considering the computational difficulty of\n   236→including all motional modes in the simulation, we only\n   237→consider the COM mode which we expect to be excited\n   238→most because the electric fields, both from the transport\n   239→and surface noise, are correlated over the whole ion string\n   240→(see Supplemental [? ]). Therefore, the average carrier\n   241→Rabi flopping can be simplified to\n   242→Pe(t) = 1\n   243→2N\n   244→[\n   245→1−\n   246→∞∑\n   247→n=0\n   248→N∑\n   249→i=0\n   250→Pn cos\n   251→(\n   252→Ω (i)\n   253→n t\n   254→)]\n   255→, (4)\n   256→wherePn is the occupation on thenth number state and\n   257→encodes a convolution between a thermal and a coher-\n   258→ent phonon distribution [? ]. Ω (i)\n   259→n is the Rabi frequency\n   260→of the ith ion on thenth number state [? ]. To ver-\n   261→ify the effectiveness of our approximation, we probe the\n   262→sideband-cooled motional spectrum of the nine-ion chain\n   263→before the tranport and verify that only the COM mode\n   264→is not cooled to near the ground state [? ], for which\n   265→we find a cooling limit of ¯nth = 4.0±3.0. We also\n   266→measure the electric-field noise induced heating and find\n   267→a heating rate of 20quanta/ms.(Fig. ??(a)), indicating\n   268→that the remaining thermal population is likely limited\n   269→by the COM mode heating which scales as ion number\n   270→N [? ]. Fig. ??(b) shows the carrier Rabi flopping after\n   271→ion transport twice as slow as in Fig.??(a). From nu-\n   272→merical simulations (blue line), we find that the data can\n   273→be explained well by a coherent state¯nα=|α|2≈50 on\n   274→the COM mode after the transport. Similarly, we per-\n   275→form the full-speed tranport and the carrier Rabi flop-\n   276→ping matches with COM coherent state with¯nα≈110\n   277→(Fig. ??(c)). As shown in the Rabi flopping plots, there\n   278→is mismatch between the experimental data and numeri-\n   279→cal simulation at full speed, which could be due to ther-\n   280→mal and coherent occupation of other modes and will\n   281→require additional investigation. For example, one can\n   282→use an individual 729nm addressing beam to probe the\n   283→blue sideband transition of different modes [? ]. The op-\n   284→timal fast transport of a long ion chain remains an open\n   285→question and is beyond the scope of this work. However,\n   286→we note that further optimization can be done by energy\n   287→self-neutral shuttling [? ? ], implementing closed-loop\n   288→optimization of the shuttling function [? ], etc.\n   289→To summarize, we have presented a multiplexed ion-\n   290→photon interface by transporting a nine-ion chain with\n   291→synchronized excitation in sub-hundred µs. The speed\n   292→is restricted by the motional frequency and can be in-\n   293→creased by an order of magnitude, for instance, using a\n   294→3D-printed ion trap [? ] with radial frequency beyond\n   295→10 MHz. The 397nm photon can be converted to the\n   296→telecommunication band via a two-step QFC [? ]. Once\n   297→integrated with state preparation on32D3/2 Zeeman sub-\n   298→level and photon collection with a single mode fiber, we\n   299→expect a faster photon extraction rate [? ] and negligi-\n   300→ble ion crosstalk while achieving high fidelity ion-photon\n   301→entanglement [ ? ? ]. Our system can also be combined\n   302→with a miniature cavity [? ] for much higher photon\n   303→extraction efficiency without sacrificing the photon gen-\n   304→eration rate, while the ion’s positional spread caused by\n   305→coherent excitation can be mitigated by aligning the cav-\n   306→ity along the radial direction or further optimization of\n   307→the shuttling function. These results stimulate the re-\n   308→search of fast shuttling of a chain of tens of ions as a\n   309→unit cell of logical qubit with heralded entanglement [?\n   310→] and high-rates entanglement of quantum processors\n   311→across large distances.\n   312→B.Y and Q.W have contributed equally to this work.\n   313→We thank Alp Sipahigil, Ben Lanyon, Tracy Northup,\n   314→You-Wei Hsieh, and Wenji Wu f